<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shoshenq I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 943–922 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a> · Also known as Sheshonq I, Shishak</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shoshenq I was a chief of the Libyan Meshwesh who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> around 943 BCE and founded the Twenty-second Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, succeeding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psusennes-ii">Psusennes II</a> peacefully. He reunified Egypt by installing his son Iuput as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/high-priest-of-amun">High Priest of Amun</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, ending the independent Theban <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theocracy">theocracy</a>. His campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> is recorded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> and is usually linked with the biblical Shishak.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shoshenq-i.jpg" alt="Shoshenq I" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ippolito Rosellini (1800-1843) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shoshenq_I_by_Rosellini.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Shoshenq I was the son of Nimlot A, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-chief-of-the-ma">Great Chief of the Ma</a>, and Tentsepeh A, the daughter of another Great Chief of the Ma, and was of Meshwesh ancestry. His uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osorkon-the-elder">Osorkon the Elder</a>, also a Meshwesh, had served on the throne as regent during the preceding 21st Dynasty, so his rise was not wholly unexpected. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psusennes-ii">Psusennes II</a> he was chief adviser to the king and Commander-in-Chief of the army, and he held his father's title of Great Chief of the Ma, the Egyptian word for the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libyans">Libyans</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osorkon-i">Osorkon I</a> married Psusennes II's daughter Maatkare B. His ancestors had settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, probably at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herakleopolis-magna">Herakleopolis Magna</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a> claims he came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bubastis">Bubastis</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Libyan ideas of rule allowed related leaders to hold power side by side, and Shoshenq used this to consolidate control. He ended the hereditary succession of the high priesthood of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> and appointed men to it, most often his own sons. His eldest son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osorkon-i">Osorkon I</a>, was chosen as his successor; his second, Iuput A, became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/high-priest-of-amun">High Priest of Amun</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> and Commander of the Army; and his third, Nimlot B, became leader of the army at Herakleopolis. A quarry inscription at Gebel el-Silsila dated to his twenty-first year records stone being cut for a great pylon and festival hall at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> to celebrate his first campaign of victory.</p><p>Late in his reign he pursued an aggressive foreign policy. A statue base from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> and a fragment of a stela from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-megiddo">Megiddo</a> bear his name, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bubastite-portal">Bubastite Portal</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> lists conquered places in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, Philistia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negev">Negev</a> and Israel, among them Megiddo, Taanach and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shechem">Shechem</a>. It is the first military action outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> formally commemorated for several centuries. He is generally presumed to be the Shishak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, who took treasure from the temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in the fifth year of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rehoboam">Rehoboam</a>, but the Karnak list does not include Jerusalem, and scholars offer different explanations for the gap.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Shoshenq I reigned for 21 years and was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osorkon-i">Osorkon I</a>. His tomb has not been found. A canopic chest of unknown origin was given to the Egyptian Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 1891, and Egyptologists have suggested that he was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanis">Tanis</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bubastis">Bubastis</a> or at the temple of Ptah at Memphis. The dates of his reign are disputed. Kenneth Kitchen's conventional dates are 945 to 924 BCE, while an astronomical reading of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakhla">Dakhla</a> stela by Rolf Krauss gives his first year as 943 BCE, the date now often used.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shoshenq I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…end of Egyptian rule in the Levant in the twelfth century BCE it survived the upheavals that destroyed cities such as Ugarit, and in the tenth century its kings were still prosperous enough to dedicate inscribed statues of the pharaohs <b>Shoshenq I</b> and Osorkon I to their goddess.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…Books of Kings, an assembly at Shechem asked Solomon’s son Rehoboam to lighten the forced labour and taxes of his father’s reign; when he refused, the northerners made Jeroboam I their king, a former overseer of Solomon’s labour gangs who had taken refuge in Egypt with Pharaoh <b>Shishak</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A few years after the division, around 925 BCE, the pharaoh <b>Shoshenq I</b>, the biblical Shishak, campaigned in the region; the Bible says Rehoboam bought him off with the treasures of the temple and palace.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Kingdom of Judah · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…official in charge of forced labour for the house of Joseph, rebelled and fled to Egypt, where he was protected by the pharaoh <b>Shishak</b>, usually identified with Shoshenq I. When Solomon died around 930 BCE, his son Rehoboam went to Shechem to be acclaimed by the northern tribes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-israel">United Kingdom of Israel · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>From these settlers came <b>Sheshonq I</b>, who founded the Twenty-second Dynasty of Egypt around 943 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/meshwesh">Meshwesh</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 943 BCE <b>Sheshonq I</b> of the Twenty-second Dynasty of Egypt installed his own son Iuput as High Priest, ending the independent theocracy.</blockquote><a href="/state/theocracy-of-amun-at-thebes">Theocracy of Amun at Thebes</a></li></ul></section>
